    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d376">

                <pb facs="0408" n="318" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg318"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/2441b</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Morris</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-07-30T20:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 30, 1918,
                                8 p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>We have just been advised by the Japanese Embassy here as follows:</p>
                <p>It has been reported that the Bolsheviki north of Khabarovsk are showing signs of
                    great unrest under the guidance of German officers and that they are preparing
                    in the Amur River, presumably in the upper currents, boats with the inferred
                    intention of sending them to sea, and that the Japanese Government has further
                    heard that there are disorders at Nikolaevsk at the mouth of the Amur River and
                    that the Japanese Government had sent some torpedo boats to protect Japanese
                    interests in Nikolaevsk.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Peking.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
